\f0\b\fs32 \cf0 Harbor and ArgoCD Deployment Notes\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 Harbor: Secret Management and Just-in-Time Environment Variables\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Harbor requires six secrets. Each 
\f2 harborConfiguration
\f1  secret field supports two modes:\
\pard\tx360\pardeftab720\li360\fi-360\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \'95	
\f0\b Plain-text in JSON
\f1\b0  \'96 Value is used as-is. Not recommended for production secrets in version-controlled files.\
\pard\tx360\pardeftab720\li360\fi-360\sa180\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \'95	
\f4\b $env:VARNAME
\f0  reference
\f1\b0  \'96 Resolved from the named environment variable at runtime. If the variable is not set when the script runs, you are prompted interactively (masked input) during the pre-flight phase \'97 before any deployment step begins. The entered value is stored in the process environment for the remainder of the run so each variable is prompted for at most once.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Setting environment variables on Windows before running (recommended for automation):
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 $env:HARBOR_ADMIN_PASSWORD = "MyAdminPassword1"\uc0\u8232 $env:SECRET_KEY             = "MySecretKey12345"   # Must be exactly 16 characters (AES-128)\u8232 $env:DATABASE_PASSWORD      = "MyDatabasePw1"\u8232 $env:CORE_SECRET_KEY        = "MyCoreSecret1"\u8232 $env:JOBSERVICE_SECRET_KEY  = "MyJobSvcSecret1"\u8232 $env:REGISTRY_SECRET_KEY    = "MyRegSecret1"\u8232 Start-ModernEdgeAtScale -EdgeSite "site1"\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 If env vars are not pre-set,
\f1\b0  the script prompts at the start of the run (before connecting to vCenter):\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 [WARNING] Environment variable "HARBOR_ADMIN_PASSWORD" (harborConfiguration.harborAdminPassword)\uc0\u8232           is not set. Prompting for interactive input.\u8232 Enter value for harborConfiguration.harborAdminPassword (env:HARBOR_ADMIN_PASSWORD): ****\u8232 \u8232 [WARNING] Environment variable "SECRET_KEY" (harborConfiguration.secretKey) is not set.\u8232           Prompting for interactive input.\u8232 Enter value for harborConfiguration.secretKey (env:SECRET_KEY): ****************\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f4\b \cf0 secretKey
\f0  length:
\f1\b0  Must be exactly 
\f0\b 16 characters
\f1\b0  (AES-128). This constraint is enforced at the point of entry \'97 whether the value came from a pre-set env var or interactive input:\
\pard\tx360\pardeftab720\li360\fi-360\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \'95	
\f0\b Pre-set env var with wrong length:
\f1\b0  An error is logged, the variable is cleared, and you are prompted to enter a corrected 16-character value before deployment proceeds.\
\'95	
\f0\b Interactive input with wrong length:
\f1\b0  The error is shown immediately and the prompt repeats until a valid 16-character value is entered (or Ctrl+C to abort).\
\pard\tx360\pardeftab720\li360\fi-360\sa180\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \'95	
\f0\b Plain-text value in JSON with wrong length:
\f1\b0  Caught at pre-flight validation (
\f2 -ValidateOnly
\f1  or at the start of any run) before any deployment begins.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Example session when 
\f2 SECRET_KEY
\f1  is set to an 8-character value:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 [ERROR] Environment variable "SECRET_KEY" (harborConfiguration.secretKey) has 8 character(s)\uc0\u8232         but must be exactly 16.\u8232 Would you like to re-enter harborConfiguration.secretKey? (Y/N): Y\u8232 [WARNING] Prompting for corrected value for harborConfiguration.secretKey (env:SECRET_KEY).\u8232 Enter value for harborConfiguration.secretKey (env:SECRET_KEY): ________\u8232 [ERROR] harborConfiguration.secretKey must be exactly 16 character(s) but the entered value\u8232         is 8 character(s).\u8232 Would you like to re-enter harborConfiguration.secretKey? (Y/N): Y\u8232 Enter value for harborConfiguration.secretKey (env:SECRET_KEY): ________________\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf0 Choosing 
\f0\b N
\f1\b0  at the 
\f2 (Y/N)
\f1  prompt aborts the deployment cleanly before any vCenter connection or deployment step:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 Would you like to re-enter harborConfiguration.secretKey? (Y/N): N\uc0\u8232 [ERROR] User chose not to re-enter harborConfiguration.secretKey. Aborting deployment.\u8232 Exception: Deployment aborted: harborConfiguration.secretKey must be exactly 16 character(s).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 TLS certificate and private key validation:
\f1\b0  
\f2 tlsCrt
\f1  and 
\f2 caCrt
\f1  must contain PEM certificates (
\f2 -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----
\f1 ). 
\f2 tlsKey
\f1  must contain a PEM private key. The script validates the first line of each file before deployment and produces a clear error if the types are swapped (a common mistake when the 
\f2 .crt
\f1  and 
\f2 .key
\f1  file paths are accidentally transposed).\

\f0\b Debug log redaction:
\f1\b0  When running with 
\f2 -LogLevel DEBUG
\f1 , all secret values in the YAML dump are replaced with 
\f2 [REDACTED]
\f1 . The private key PEM block (
\f2 tls.key
\f1 ) is redacted line-by-line. The TLS certificate (
\f2 tls.crt
\f1 ) is left visible as it is public material. The key name and YAML structure remain intact for structural debugging.\

\f0\b Saving the Harbor data values file (
\f4 -SaveHarborYaml
\f0 ):
\f1\b0  By default, the temporary Harbor data values YAML is deleted after successful installation. Specify 
\f2 -SaveHarborYaml
\f1  to move it to a 
\f2 HarborYaml
\f1  subdirectory under the module directory instead:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 Start-ModernEdgeAtScale -SaveHarborYaml\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf0 The directory is created automatically. If it cannot be created, deployment exits with an error before Harbor installation begins.\
\pard\pardeftab720\li720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Security warning:
\f1\b0  The saved file contains 
\f0\b Harbor passwords and secrets in plain text
\f1\b0 . A 
\f2 [WARNING]
\f1  is logged whenever this switch is used. A redacted copy is available in the deployment log. Treat the 
\f2 HarborYaml
\f1  directory like a credentials store \'97 restrict access, exclude it from source control, and 
\f0\b delete the file immediately
\f1\b0  after resolving the issue.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Harbor credentials display:
\f1\b0  After a successful Harbor deployment, the script always prints the Harbor login details (URL, admin username, and password) directly to the console, regardless of the configured 
\f2 LogLevel
\f1 . This ensures credentials are never silently swallowed even when running at a high log level.\

\f0\b Supervisor container image registry registration:
\f1\b0  After Harbor succeeds, the module registers it on the Supervisor (
\f0\b Supervisor 
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  Configure 
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  Container Registries
\f1\b0  in vCenter) using the Harbor VIP, admin login, and optional 
\f2 caCrt
\f1 , and sets it as the default registry.\
On 
\f2 -CleanUp Harbor
\f1 , 
\f2 -CleanUp Supervisor
\f1 , or 
\f2 -CleanUp All
\f1 , that registration is removed before the Harbor service uninstall. Failures there log as warnings; finish the step in vCenter if needed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 ArgoCD: Deployment Output, Namespace Uniqueness, and Fallback\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Deployment output:
\f1\b0  After a successful ArgoCD instance deployment, the script logs the initial admin credentials and provides the command to change them:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 [INFO] Login as user "admin" using temporary password: vEHJgoj3wGtHQDlI\uc0\u8232 [INFO] To update your password run: "argocd.exe account update-password\u8232        --server 10.40.12.203 --account admin --insecure"\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf0 Change the password immediately after deployment using the provided command.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Namespace uniqueness:
\f1\b0  The ArgoCD namespace in 
\f2 argocd-deployment.yml
\f1  is automatically suffixed with the cluster MoRef (e.g. 
\f2 argocd
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  
\f2 argocd-c462
\f1 ). This prevents conflicts when multiple supervisor clusters share the same vCenter. The base namespace in 
\f2 argocd-deployment.yml
\f1  must match 
\f2 supervisorServices.nameSpacePrefix
\f1  in 
\f2 infrastructure.json
\f1 ; the suffix is appended automatically.\

\f0\b ArgoCD rollback and retry:
\f1\b0  If ArgoCD deployment fails (pods do not reach ready state, instance timeout, or YAML misconfiguration), rollback removes only the ArgoCD namespace. The supervisor and Harbor remain intact. To fix the YAML and retry:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 # 1. Remove only the ArgoCD namespace; supervisor and Harbor remain intact.\uc0\u8232 Start-ModernEdgeAtScale -CleanUp ArgoCD -EdgeSite "site1"\u8232 \u8232 # 2. Fix your argocd-deployment.yml, then re-run.\u8232 #    Supervisor and Harbor steps are idempotently skipped.\u8232 Start-ModernEdgeAtScale -EdgeSite "site1"\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Harbor rollback and retry:
\f1\b0  Harbor failure rolls back only the Harbor Supervisor Service. The supervisor and ArgoCD remain intact and are idempotently verified (not re-deployed) on retry:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 # 1. If you declined the rollback prompt or the service is stuck in ERROR state:\uc0\u8232 Start-ModernEdgeAtScale -CleanUp Harbor -EdgeSite "site1"\u8232 \u8232 # 2. Fix the issue (see Troubleshooting below), then retry.\u8232 #    Supervisor and ArgoCD steps are idempotently skipped.\u8232 Start-ModernEdgeAtScale -EdgeSite "site1"\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Autonomous (unattended) runs:
\f1\b0  Use 
\f2 -RollbackOnFailure $true
\f1  to roll back automatically on any failure without prompting. Use 
\f2 -RollbackOnFailure $false
\f1  to skip rollback and continue to the next site. Omit the parameter for an interactive Y/N/Always prompt at each failure.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

